00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:45.840
All right.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.200
So let's talk about
the 11 or 12 factors

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.810
of a productive software team.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.540
Number one, the
[INAUDIBLE] motto.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.220
Build something people want.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.420
It doesn't matter how
productive you are.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.420
You can make things
lightning fast.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.450
If you build things that are
useless, that nobody wants,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.990
that don't have product market
fit, that is the biggest

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.100
productivity suck.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.770
Essentially, your
productivity is zero

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.240
if you build things that
nobody actually uses.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.750
How do you do that?

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.769
Well, it's actually
not too hard.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:22.029
Talk to customers
before you build things.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:25.550
There's a ton of documentation
on this on the web.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.175
Talk to people.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.640
Emailing people doesn't count.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.960
You might email folks.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.760
It's not the same medium.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.030
It is amazingly low bandwidth
versus actually talking

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:36.960
to people.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.510
I actually like to think of it--

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.640
if you've ever seen
"Gorillas in the Mist."

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.980
Jane Goodall, she
was in the jungle

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.510
observing the chimpanzees.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:50.282
And that's what you want to do
to figure out what you want.

00:04:50.282 --> 00:04:52.740
Whether you're an enterprise
company or a consumer company,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.590
go to your target
audience's native habitat.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.030
Watch what they do.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:58.830
See the problems they encounter.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.998
Don't ask them about solutions.

00:05:00.998 --> 00:05:02.790
If you understand the
problems really well,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.500
then you can make good
solutions for them.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.180
And to me, that is the
key to making things

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.263
that people actually want.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.865
So this is actually something
that one of my colleagues

00:05:13.865 --> 00:05:14.490
at Amazon said.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:15.870
I thought it was the
funniest thing ever.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.310
I added the parentheses here.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.310
Premature optimization
is the root of all evil.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:25.020
This happens again and again
and again in software orgs.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.030
You'll say, well, we
can't do it this way

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.410
because that won't scale.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.440
Well, who cares?

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.778
Do the fast thing
that doesn't scale.

00:05:34.778 --> 00:05:36.820
This way you can test out
if what you're building

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.010
is actually useful for people.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:42.837
Don't solve problems that you
don't have yet because that

00:05:42.837 --> 00:05:45.170
means-- if you don't have the
problem yet that means you

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:46.460
don't understand the problem.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.230
You might think you're solving
a scaling problem you're

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.790
going to hit in six
months, but odds

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.123
are in six months the actual
scaling problems you'll hit

00:05:52.123 --> 00:05:54.110
will be completely different.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:58.490
Or if you are right, you'll
understand it even better.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.670
And sort of corollary to
that, don't plan for scaling

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:03.170
more than 10x past
where you are,

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:06.050
even if you're in
hyper growth mode.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.200
If you're growing double
digit percentages every month

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.390
in terms of the usage
of your product--

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:14.120
presumably software
product-- you

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.820
might want to plan for
three months down the line

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.740
when you're 50% bigger.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:21.230
But don't plan for when
you're 10x bigger because it's

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.390
really hard to imagine that.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.510
Just keep doing those three
month, six month things.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.260
Another great way to avoid
premature optimization

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:37.760
that I've seen work in
various forms is sneaker wear.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.340
You might have some
really, really hard problem

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.800
and it's going to take a
ton of time to solve it.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.980
But if you can solve
it by just faking it

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:48.230
that works really well.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:52.820
A story there that
illustrates that.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:56.310
When I first got to Heroku,
we had this dedicated database

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:56.810
product.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:58.020
There was a shared
database product

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.960
where you got one
database on a big cluster

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.810
and we could spin those
up programmatically

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.350
in a couple of seconds, and
that's what most people used.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.347
But if you wanted to pay the
massive amount of $200 a month

00:07:07.347 --> 00:07:08.930
you could get this
dedicated database.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:11.305
You had a whole virtual
machine to yourself.

00:07:11.305 --> 00:07:13.930
So it turns out if you went into
the interface, you clicked it,

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.100
you'd get a message that
says your database will be

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.997
available in 24 hours or less.

00:07:17.997 --> 00:07:20.330
What really happened was it
emailed one of the engineers

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.110
at Heroku.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.990
They went into the AWS console,
they spun up a virtual machine,

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:27.110
then they went in and manually
configured your app to use it.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.500
That worked great
for the first year

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.380
there when we had
about a couple hundred

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:34.825
of those dedicated databases.

00:07:34.825 --> 00:07:36.950
Pretty soon, we were seeing
that it wasn't scaling.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:38.250
That's when we fixed it.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:41.520
But if we had said, no,
no, no, we need to scale.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.860
We need to plan for the
future, the whole product

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.937
would've been out
12 months later,

00:07:47.937 --> 00:07:50.270
and we wouldn't have learned
a whole lot of other things

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.020
that we learned.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:55.100
So don't prematurely optimize.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.190
This is basically the opposite.

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:00.020
How do you avoid
premature optimization?

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:01.670
It's so hard to do.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:03.350
Even if you're just
writing some code

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.857
you might be adding
some extra tables

00:08:05.857 --> 00:08:07.440
to your database or
some extra fields.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.232
You'll be like, well,
I don't need this now

00:08:09.232 --> 00:08:11.810
but I'm going to need it later,
and it doesn't cost anything.

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:14.360
Still costs mental
bandwidth, still takes time,

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.130
still has the opportunity
to create bugs.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.280
So a really great way to
avoid premature optimization

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:20.930
is something called a spike.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:22.733
That's where you
build something.

00:08:22.733 --> 00:08:24.900
You give yourself some
really short amount of time--

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.060
one week or two weeks--

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:28.250
and you tell
yourself or your team

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:30.830
we are not going to
use any of the code

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:32.360
that we write this week.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.940
We are purely doing this
just to test to make sure

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.970
that we can build some
software that actually works,

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:40.669
that bears some
resemblance to the app

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:43.010
we ultimately want to make.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.300
What's great is after
you make a spike,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.690
you can try it out with
customers, get their feedback.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:52.580
You also may encounter some of
the hard technical challenges

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:54.080
in actually building the thing.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.460
You might try to do something.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.700
Make a dedicated database
and you realize, oh, man.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.870
That's really,
really, really hard.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.800
And so it's a method of
discovering what's hard

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.450
and what's not
and testing to see

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.600
if the thing you're going
to build is actually useful.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:12.920
Often, I've seen teams plan
to do two or three spikes,

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:18.620
basically a week or two each,
and then go on and build

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:19.640
the actual thing.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.848
Sometimes you'll discover
that your spike is actually

00:09:21.848 --> 00:09:24.050
totally good enough to
be the actual product,

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.045
and isn't that awesome?

00:09:25.045 --> 00:09:26.420
You've got something
in two weeks

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.985
that you can then
actually start deploying.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.980
Another thing.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.900
I found that really proactive
organizations are generally

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.970
shipping really
good quality things.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:42.680
It feels great to
ship something good,

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:47.480
it feels really bad
to ship something bad,

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.800
and it's really,
really hard to do.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:55.100
I think we sort of have this
prototype in software of Steve

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:58.550
Jobs, who's the quality tyrant.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:02.330
He was famous for
berating people,

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:06.020
when the quality wasn't
up to his standards,

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.390
about how bad a job
they were doing.

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:12.410
So I think the trick
here is you want

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.770
to be ruthless about
quality but you never

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:18.290
attack the person for something
that's not high quality.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:22.040
You talk about the
product that was created.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.180
You assume good intentions and
you don't make it personal,

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:28.520
but you still have a really,
really high quality bar.

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.645
And I think ultimately,
everybody will feel really,

00:10:30.645 --> 00:10:33.230
really good about
building good things,

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:35.150
and that actually
really helps quite a bit

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.640
for having a productive org.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:42.070
All right, next.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.750
Start with the monolith.

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:47.470
Microservices is all
the rage these days.

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:49.150
You read a ton about it.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:53.050
You'll read blog posts about
my microservices architecture

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:56.760
for building the next dog
food selling e-commerce site.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:07.420
Actually, at one of the
places I was at the team

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.120
was building some web services--

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:10.600
not going to name names--

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:13.860
and they were living in
the microservices world

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.360
so they decided to make it a
microservices app from day one.

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:18.790
It had five or six
different services.

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:20.230
It was a total mess.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:28.180
This is really just a corollary
to the premature optimization.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.760
What happened in that
case was the team

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:33.920
was solving problems that
they didn't understand.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:35.740
They thought the
microservices architecture

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:37.090
would solve things really well.

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:39.760
It actually made
everything harder.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:40.840
So it's really simple.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:42.430
Start off with a
single code base,

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:46.400
and then as you discover
what's wrong you split it off,

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:48.280
and I think that's
actually the next slide.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.758
So if you do start
with a monolith,

00:11:53.758 --> 00:11:55.550
does that mean you stay
a monolith forever?

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:56.850
No.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.020
That's what PayPal
did and eBay did.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:03.030
That's what slowed them down
and let Amazon overtake them

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:04.540
15 years ago.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.300
It's almost hard to remember
but back then Amazon and PayPal

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:13.080
were like neck and neck,
or Amazon and eBay.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:15.120
So you don't stay
a monolith forever.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:19.500
You split out services as you
discover your scaling problems,

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:22.890
and those scaling problems
can be organizational

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:25.800
or they can be technical.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.410
For example, one pattern
I've seen over and over

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:31.740
and over again is some software
is getting some really, really

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:37.715
good uptake and it's starting to
hit some performance problems.

00:12:37.715 --> 00:12:39.090
And when you look
at it, it might

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:43.110
be the database that is causing
the performance problems,

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:47.310
but it's like one table is
95% of all the databases load,

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:49.320
or 98%.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:53.040
At Heroku we had a
group messaging app.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.710
Basically, their whole
reason to [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:55.710 --> 00:13:00.070
was that you couldn't send
group text messages on iPhones.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.055
It's kind of crazy to remember.

00:13:02.055 --> 00:13:04.180
So they had this group
messaging app to solve that.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:05.830
It worked really, really well.

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.480
They started as a monolith.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:10.030
They had a Postgres
database and there

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:12.440
was a messages
table that was 98%

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:13.690
of the load on their database.

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:15.273
And it pretty quickly
got to the point

00:13:15.273 --> 00:13:19.090
where they couldn't scale
up a single database larger

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:20.530
than what they had.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.230
They were using the very
biggest database out there.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:25.780
There were a lot of other
data stores besides Postgres

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:26.830
that would have
been great for them

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:27.980
and that were great for them.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:29.840
So what they did, they
split out of service.

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:32.510
It was a messaging
service that it

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:36.610
was backed by a database that
was ideal for that workload.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:41.150
And boom, they solved the
problem and they moved on.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:48.160
Another common scaling
thing is the team.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.200
I'm a believer in
the two pizza rule.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.630
If you can't feed your
team on two pizzas

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:54.850
then it might be time
to split it apart,

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:58.570
or can everybody fit in
the conference room thing.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:04.270
Whatever the end law is of
the number of connections

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:06.062
in the team, it becomes
really, really hard

00:14:06.062 --> 00:14:11.980
once you get past 12 or 15
people to be a cohesive team.

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:14.980
So maybe your scaling
problem is organizational.

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:19.090
You split out a separate
team, and then they

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:20.230
can act as their own team.

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:21.105
It works really well.

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:30.750
I'm going to skip
to number eight

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:32.530
here because that's
correlated to this.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:38.380
So when you do split out
microservices and teams--

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:40.510
and this is really,
really important--

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:43.110
you need to make
them technically

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:46.652
and organizationally
independent.

00:14:46.652 --> 00:14:47.610
What do I mean by that?

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.820
I mean there is actually a
team behind each service.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.010
The whole notion
of a microservice

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:56.490
that kind of manages itself is,
I think, a bit of a fallacy.

00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:59.850
It does happen, but the
pattern I've seen over and over

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:01.950
and over again is you
have a microservice.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.797
It has its own team.

00:15:03.797 --> 00:15:04.380
Really simple.

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.870
The teams can be as
little as one person,

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.360
although you hope they
don't get hit by a bus,

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.832
or they could be a
full two pizza team.

00:15:11.832 --> 00:15:13.290
But this works
really, really well.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.360
That way that team can
develop its own culture.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.450
They can have their
own planning process.

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:21.090
Maybe they decide to do a two
week sprint instead of one week

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.210
sprint because that's what works
best for their own code base,

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:25.710
but they're their
own separate team.

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:32.160
From a technical
perspective, independence

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:34.610
means that the team can
deploy their own code

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.000
base independently.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.540
And this is a really
easy smell if you've

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:41.010
done microservices wrong.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:44.640
If to deploy your service
another team needs

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:46.307
to do a new deployment
of their service,

00:15:46.307 --> 00:15:48.390
and so you need to do this
weird coordinated thing

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:50.190
between the two services,
you did it wrong.

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:52.273
You're not getting the
benefits of a microservice.

00:15:52.273 --> 00:15:55.270
You just made it harder
to deploy new software.

00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:56.560
So how do you do that?

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.450
You have stable versioned
APIs that the services

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:01.830
use to talk to each other.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:06.090
Those APIs can be HTTP APIs.

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.230
They can be other APIs.

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:08.980
It doesn't matter.

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:10.740
They could just be
going on a UNIX socket,

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:13.460
but there's an API,
that thing stays stable,

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:15.210
then each of the
services can move as fast

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:17.983
as they want around that API.

00:16:17.983 --> 00:16:19.650
Occasionally, you'll
have to break that.

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:21.810
Occasionally, you
realize that API

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:23.487
doesn't have the capabilities.

00:16:23.487 --> 00:16:24.570
That's why you version it.

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:26.370
You create a new
version of the API.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:29.370
The service that's
consuming that API is still

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.600
using the old version.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:32.730
Once that new version
of the API is out there,

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.520
then it can switch over on
its own schedule to using

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:39.475
the new version of the API.

00:16:39.475 --> 00:16:42.630
I skipped over number seven.

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:47.330
So again, this is a team thing.

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:49.410
I've found that
teams work best when

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:55.260
they're either all
co-located or all remote.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:57.170
Co-location can be
a fabulous thing

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:58.950
if it works for your company.

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:02.040
You get the water
cooler conversations.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.339
It's easy to drop
by somebody's desk

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:08.819
if you need some
question answered.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:13.510
But for a lot of folks that's
not practical, in which case

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:15.240
you're in remote mode.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:16.260
Everybody's remote.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:20.069
That means every meeting
has a video conference

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:21.990
or a conference call
that they can be on.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.810
That means you over
index on writing things

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:27.099
down so that if somebody
is in a different time zone

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:29.141
and there was a meeting
they missed they can just

00:17:29.141 --> 00:17:31.485
read what happened.

00:17:31.485 --> 00:17:33.630
And essentially, if you
have a hybrid team where

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.313
you have half the
people in one location

00:17:35.313 --> 00:17:37.230
and half the people
remote, you basically just

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:39.960
need to act like
everybody's remote

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.940
and then it works pretty well.

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:46.410
But I think that's one of the
really interesting challenges

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:48.870
in terms of
productivity software.

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:53.370
How do you get remote teams
working better together?

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.340
And I'd love to see some cool
innovation there because right

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:58.673
now it's basically-- the
state-of-the-art that I've seen

00:17:58.673 --> 00:18:01.298
is you have a shared Google Doc
on a meeting so anybody can see

00:18:01.298 --> 00:18:01.980
that doc.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:04.858
You have a video conference,
and it's pretty good.

00:18:04.858 --> 00:18:07.150
Not quite the same as everybody
being in the same room,

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:08.960
but it's still pretty good.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:10.490
OK, we already did this one.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:15.880
What is this one?

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:19.810
Oh, yeah, so you started
off with a monolith.

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.670
You scaled, your product
is getting traction,

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:26.290
you started splitting into
separate microservices

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:29.380
with separate teams.

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:33.610
Now you've got a new problem.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:35.770
This is a problem that
we totally had at Heroku

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:37.890
that we did wrong.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.750
You'll have teams that might
be really, really productive

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:43.310
and they're all moving
in different directions.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:46.318
It's like they're all running
in totally different ways,

00:18:46.318 --> 00:18:47.860
and so that's not
really good either.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:52.910
So you really have to over
index on your cohesive strategy.

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:55.130
There's a lot of
different ways to do this.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:56.770
I'm a big believer,
if you can do it,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:19:00.070
in having first principles--

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:03.160
Elon Musk is a big fan of
this first principle thing.

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:04.210
What does that mean?

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:07.060
I heard this really cool quote
from a friend of a friend.

00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:09.460
He was talking to somebody
who worked at SpaceX,

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:13.360
and somebody said, what
do you do at SpaceX?

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:17.170
The way he answered
it is colonizing Mars

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:20.140
is essential to humanity's
future existence.

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:21.820
In order to do that,
we need to build

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:24.520
a spaceship powerful enough
and economical enough

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:25.630
to get us there.

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:27.850
And in order to do that,
we need to have engines

00:19:27.850 --> 00:19:29.560
that have a thrust of this.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:32.560
And so I work on the
exhaust valve of the engines

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:34.310
in order to make that happen.

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:37.540
So there's that first principle
of we need to colonize Mars,

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:41.440
and that lets that
engineer figure out

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:45.093
how to build that exhaust valve
to solve the problems that

00:19:45.093 --> 00:19:45.760
actually matter.

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:51.175
Another way to do it is--

00:19:51.175 --> 00:19:53.425
what you need when you have
a bunch of different teams

00:19:53.425 --> 00:19:58.960
is essentially just some
central strategy czar.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:00.970
Typically, I had a
product or a co-founder

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:04.120
or a CEO that just
continually says,

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:07.210
we're doing this, and making
sure people aligned to it.

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:12.460
I think when you
have separate teams

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:18.950
you want to have at least half
to 70% of their time working

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:21.270
on projects that are for
the whole organization.

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:23.000
But still give them
maybe like 20% time

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:25.730
to work on their own projects,
whether that's technical debt

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:27.740
reduction or just some
features that they think

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:30.455
would be important, because
they might have some ground

00:20:30.455 --> 00:20:32.330
truth from their
interactions with the market

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.608
or their software
development that they

00:20:34.608 --> 00:20:35.900
realize something is important.

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:36.890
It's not important
to the whole org

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:38.690
but it's important
to their part of it,

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:42.260
so give those separate
teams their 20% or 40%

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:44.990
time to work on separate,
independent projects as well.

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:52.980
Like they say in politics,
it's the economy, stupid.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:57.300
I think in organizations
it's the culture.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:01.560
It's sort of trite,
but it's really true.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:05.310
You want to create a place
that people enjoy working at.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:08.882
They enjoy hanging out
with their colleagues.

00:21:08.882 --> 00:21:10.215
That will get you through a lot.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:14.790
Confession.

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:16.110
I went to business
school, and you

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:17.943
do a lot of case studies
in business school.

00:21:17.943 --> 00:21:21.480
And one of my meta takeaways,
when you read-- all the cases

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.370
are always about
successful companies.

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:26.658
And in almost every
successful company

00:21:26.658 --> 00:21:28.200
in one of these case
studies, there's

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:30.825
always some really interesting,
unique factor in their culture.

00:21:33.981 --> 00:21:37.950
It's kind of like another way
of having that strategy that

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:40.860
helps different teams figure
out what they need to do.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:47.090
And last but not
least, use serverless.

00:21:47.090 --> 00:21:48.590
This is a talk about
serverless so I

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:49.790
couldn't have left this out.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:52.010
But it really is
true, and that's

00:21:52.010 --> 00:21:55.190
what I'm going to
talk about next.

00:21:55.190 --> 00:21:57.320
And by serverless I don't
mean just functions.

00:21:57.320 --> 00:22:01.230
I mean platforms as a service,
app-based serverless things,

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.230
container-based serverless
things like Cloud Run

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:03.980
that we launched.

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:05.870
But serverless
really, really, really

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:07.850
can be a giant
productivity boost.

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:10.310
You are eliminating
so much of the stuff

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:14.900
that you would normally have
to do in running software.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:17.930
It's not for everything,
but for a lot of software

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:20.400
it is really great.

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:23.730
And so that brings us
to the next section.

00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:25.700
So why serverless
and why App Engine?

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.490
Obviously I'm doing a lot of
creative borrowing on my deck

00:22:34.490 --> 00:22:35.740
here.

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:38.280
If you've ever seen
Maslow's hierarchy of needs,

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:44.140
this is like the
hierarchy of computing.

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:48.250
At the lowest level
you have the on prem,

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:51.100
you're running your
own data center world.

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:55.150
Even before PayPal I
started off in this role.

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:58.270
I was doing a startup and I
rented a rack in a data center,

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:01.060
and I went down and I racked
these physical servers.

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:02.620
And then I installed
Oracle on them,

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:03.970
and it felt really, really cool.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:05.512
I was dealing with
thyis heavy metal.

00:23:05.512 --> 00:23:07.480
But it's a lot of
work, actually.

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:10.840
You have to deal with physical
security, network hardware,

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:13.303
physical servers,
them going down,

00:23:13.303 --> 00:23:15.220
the internet connectivity,
even if you're just

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:18.850
renting out a data center.

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:21.820
Most of that stuff
isn't differentiated.

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:24.370
If you're differentiating
on how you rack servers--

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:25.460
maybe you are.

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:31.583
I mean, if you're building
some crazy infrastructure that

00:23:31.583 --> 00:23:32.750
is a place to differentiate.

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:35.042
I mean, I think that's really
where the cloud providers

00:23:35.042 --> 00:23:39.070
differentiate is how they
operate that layer, or even

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:40.990
Google outside of the cloud.

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:42.880
Google's done a lot of
cool, special things

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:47.380
in building these really
amazing data centers that

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:49.670
let us operate at global scale.

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:52.270
So you can innovate it there
but most companies don't.

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:55.002
So the next layer up is cloud,
and cloud is really just

00:23:55.002 --> 00:23:57.460
where you take all the physical
stuff out but you're really

00:23:57.460 --> 00:23:58.480
doing the same things.

00:23:58.480 --> 00:23:59.563
You have virtual machines.

00:23:59.563 --> 00:24:00.975
You have virtual networks.

00:24:00.975 --> 00:24:02.350
You deal with
cluster management.

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:04.780
You still deal with OS updates.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:07.030
Again, how many people do they--

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:10.360
how many people do you feel
that your companies are

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:13.090
differentiating based on how
well you update the operating

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:15.040
systems on your
virtual machines?

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:17.920
Maybe, but I doubt it.

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:20.572
So the next level
up is serverless,

00:24:20.572 --> 00:24:22.780
and that's where you get
rid of all this other stuff.

00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:24.683
You just focus on
application development.

00:24:24.683 --> 00:24:27.100
In an ideal world it would be
just application development

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:27.220
here.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:28.762
You just write the
code for your app.

00:24:28.762 --> 00:24:31.907
In reality, you have to still
monitor the performance.

00:24:31.907 --> 00:24:34.240
You're going to run into some
scaling issues eventually.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:35.936
You need to do some
re-architecture so you

00:24:35.936 --> 00:24:37.061
need to keep track of that.

00:24:37.061 --> 00:24:40.520
But really, you're just focusing
on developing your software,

00:24:40.520 --> 00:24:42.250
your code, your business logic.

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:44.770
That's where I think
90% of companies

00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:47.080
differentiate these days.

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:49.370
And then Maslow's
hierarchy of needs.

00:24:49.370 --> 00:24:52.420
Once you've met all the needs,
like eating and sleeping

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:55.570
and all those other things,
you have self-actualization.

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:58.152
And so I think if you
do all these things

00:24:58.152 --> 00:24:59.860
you can self-actualize
and actually build

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:01.590
great products,
which is hopefully

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:04.350
what we all want to do.

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:06.840
And I think as you go
up the stack obviously

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:08.610
you get more productivity.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:10.150
The less other
stuff you have to do

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:13.240
the more you focus on
the stuff that matters,

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:15.340
so that's why serverless.

00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:17.800
Now we're going to shift into
the other part of the talk.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.830
I kind of mentioned that
there's two talks in one

00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:24.790
here, which is what's
new in App Engine.

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:27.670
How many folks are
current App Engine users?

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:30.220
Have you guys tried out
the second gen runtimes?

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:31.990
Anyone?

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.840
So this is the big
thing that's new.

00:25:34.840 --> 00:25:36.340
We've been releasing
what we've been

00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:40.968
calling the second generation
App Engine runtimes since--

00:25:40.968 --> 00:25:43.010
if I stand out of the
light can you still see me?

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:44.450
It's really blaring over there.

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:44.950
Yeah?

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:45.450
Good.

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:48.550
So we released what we called
second generation run times.

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:50.500
And I wish I had
all this on a slide,

00:25:50.500 --> 00:25:52.840
but I'm just going to talk
through it a little bit.

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:56.055
App Engine of 10 years ago was--

00:25:56.055 --> 00:25:57.930
it actually, for those
of you who don't know,

00:25:57.930 --> 00:25:59.740
it predated Google Cloud.

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:01.480
Yes, question?

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:03.180
Thumbs up.

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:03.680
It's good.

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:05.200
Yeah, it is good.

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:06.500
It predated Google Cloud.

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:10.570
The reason it's not called
Google Cloud App Engine is

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:13.630
because there was no Google
Cloud when App Engine

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:16.960
[? existed. ?] I think it's one
of the only products in Google

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:21.010
Cloud that is usually referred
to as Google X, Google App

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:23.440
Engine is the common
moniker for it.

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:26.260
Almost everything
else is Cloud--

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:27.640
Cloud Spanner,
Cloud Memorystore,

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:29.050
Cloud Compute Engine.

00:26:31.732 --> 00:26:33.190
And it was this
all-in-one product.

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:35.620
Because there was no
rest of the cloud,

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:37.270
you've got everything
all together.

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:40.300
You had data store
for storing your data.

00:26:40.300 --> 00:26:44.140
You had MemcacheG which was
a Google proprietary variant

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:45.160
of Memcache.

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:46.630
You had a search API.

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:48.880
You had a Users API for
managing your users.

00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:52.170
Is anyone using the Users API?

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:54.580
It's great, but if you were
building an app today--

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:56.170
so for those of
you who don't know,

00:26:56.170 --> 00:26:57.760
the Users API--
literally you can

00:26:57.760 --> 00:27:00.790
use an API that will
manage your user

00:27:00.790 --> 00:27:05.110
data so you don't need to have
a users table in your app.

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:06.957
But you would never
build an app that way

00:27:06.957 --> 00:27:08.290
if you're building from scratch.

00:27:08.290 --> 00:27:10.928
You would just
have a users table.

00:27:10.928 --> 00:27:12.970
There's a ton of libraries
in every language that

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:17.950
help you manage users
and that's how you do it,

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:22.520
but that was what was built.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:25.240
There were a bunch of other
restrictions because--

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:27.130
and this isn't
widely publicized,

00:27:27.130 --> 00:27:29.200
but because it
predated Google Cloud,

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:31.960
Google App Engine was
and still does runs

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:37.750
in the same Borg cluster as
the rest of Google proper.

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:41.440
It's running right
alongside of Search and Ads

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:44.500
and all those
other products that

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:47.810
make the real money for Google.

00:27:47.810 --> 00:27:52.520
And so App Engine, as a
result, was super locked down.

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:54.490
You didn't even have
direct network access.

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:57.770
You could not just call
out to a network address,

00:27:57.770 --> 00:28:01.690
so you had to use this
API called URL Fetch.

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:03.850
And you'd call
the URL Fetch API,

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:06.610
and then some other
service would go fetch

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:08.570
what you were looking for.

00:28:08.570 --> 00:28:11.625
So it was just a very
funky, funky environment.

00:28:11.625 --> 00:28:13.750
So what we've done on the
second generation runtime

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:15.370
since we started
releasing last year

00:28:15.370 --> 00:28:19.900
is we've basically defunked it.

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:22.900
We took out those
proprietary APIs.

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:24.010
There's no Users API.

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:25.490
You just do the users yourself.

00:28:25.490 --> 00:28:28.410
You have direct network access.

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:31.840
And most API client
libraries will work for you.

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:41.290
The first language on the second
generation runtimes was Node.

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:43.210
It's actually been
getting great uptake.

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:47.290
Node was not available in App
Engine standard prior to that,

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:53.440
and now we have all
these languages.

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:55.930
A couple of these
were out before.

00:28:55.930 --> 00:28:58.630
A lot of them we just
announced at the conference.

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:04.750
So Google Cloud has seven
languages that we support.

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:09.970
We now have six out of the
seven on the second generation

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:11.030
runtimes.

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:13.060
A couple of these are alpha.

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:13.870
It's really easy.

00:29:13.870 --> 00:29:18.505
If you want to get into the
alpha you can just ping me.

00:29:18.505 --> 00:29:21.460
We basically let everybody in
even though it's officially

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:22.270
a private alpha.

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:28.590
Maybe you've heard
about Cloud Run.

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:32.340
That was the big serverless
announcement at the conference.

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:34.330
These App Engine second
generation runtimes

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:36.497
are running on the same
infrastructure as Cloud Run.

00:29:36.497 --> 00:29:39.010
They're running actually
in Docker containers.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:42.460
And Cloud Run is, from a
simplified point of view,

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:46.720
it's just removing the App
Engine code building layer

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:50.880
and exposing the
containers directly to you.

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:52.615
So I've got all these now.

00:29:52.615 --> 00:29:53.740
These are modern languages.

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:56.330
You should try them out.

00:29:56.330 --> 00:30:01.010
And this is a you
heard it here first.

00:30:01.010 --> 00:30:02.630
On those second
generation runtimes

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:05.750
we are doubling the
memory that you get.

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:10.580
Right now the smallest instance
class you get 128 megs of RAM.

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:13.790
That hasn't changed in a
while, and what we're seeing

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:16.580
is actually that most apps,
even really simple ones,

00:30:16.580 --> 00:30:19.280
don't fit in 128 megs anymore.

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:21.620
So all the second
generation runtimes

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:23.090
are going to get double the RAM.

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.645
We'll be shipping
that this quarter.

00:30:25.645 --> 00:30:27.020
I'm crossing my
fingers that it's

00:30:27.020 --> 00:30:29.150
going to happen in
the next six weeks,

00:30:29.150 --> 00:30:32.060
but officially
sometime this quarter.

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:33.320
There'll be no price change.

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:35.778
You'll get double the memory
on second generation runtimes.

00:30:35.778 --> 00:30:37.490
So surprise, it's
going to be really fun.

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:45.130
Like I said before, and this
is really, really important,

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:48.360
the second generation runtimes
enable open network access.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:49.720
Why is that important?

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:54.390
Well, Google Cloud's
got some cool stuff.

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:56.917
There is cloud storage
for storing your blobs.

00:30:56.917 --> 00:30:58.750
That wasn't available
in the old App Engine.

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:00.060
It's available in the new one.

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:03.870
There's Cloud Spanner, where
you can have a database that's

00:31:03.870 --> 00:31:06.230
on multiple continents.

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:07.860
On the last session,
I demoed an app

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:11.802
that was running on multiple
continents using Spanner.

00:31:11.802 --> 00:31:14.010
But without open network
access you couldn't actually

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:15.390
use any of those APIs.

00:31:15.390 --> 00:31:17.700
So now with open
network access you

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:19.710
can just use the Google
Cloud client library

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:25.770
and access those
Google APIs directly.

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:27.305
But like I said--

00:31:27.305 --> 00:31:30.150
and this is where the
product's a little complicated.

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:33.750
Because App Engine is still
running in that main Google

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:40.080
Borg cluster, it actually
is not on the same network

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:44.130
as the virtual machines
that you might spin up

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:45.870
in Google Cloud proper.

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:47.970
And a lot of the
products in Google Cloud,

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:50.580
like notably Cloud
Memorystore, are

00:31:50.580 --> 00:31:52.030
based on the virtual machines.

00:31:52.030 --> 00:31:55.890
So besides network access,
the really, really big thing

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:59.400
that we announced
here is VPC Connect.

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:02.230
So Google Cloud has VPCs.

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:03.360
I'm not a networking guy.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:05.990
Network, to me, is
like magic pixie dust,

00:32:05.990 --> 00:32:08.280
but what's cool is
it really is magic.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:08.920
It works.

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:13.050
So this is a really
cool town in France.

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:16.925
The road to it gets under
water on very high tides.

00:32:16.925 --> 00:32:19.050
It's just like a little
jewel box [INAUDIBLE] town.

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:22.620
So this is App Engine
before VPC Connect.

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:24.263
It was just this little island.

00:32:24.263 --> 00:32:26.430
Really great if you had
what you want on the island.

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:29.700
But with VPC Connect, we've
got a bridge from the island

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:31.900
to the mainland.

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:33.250
Kind of a cute, little analogy.

00:32:33.250 --> 00:32:36.640
But the important
thing here is you can

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:39.753
have your VMs in Google Cloud.

00:32:39.753 --> 00:32:41.170
The number one
feature that's been

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:43.330
asked for on the second
generation runtimes

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:44.540
has been a caching service--

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:46.000
Memcache or Redis.

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:49.540
So you can spin up
your Redis instance,

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:52.600
and later on when it's
available a Memcache instance.

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:55.030
It's in a VPC.

00:32:55.030 --> 00:32:57.910
You create a VPC
connector in App Engine

00:32:57.910 --> 00:33:03.370
and you just address it on
its internal 10.0.-whatever

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:06.010
address, and it works.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:08.830
And actually, that was--

00:33:08.830 --> 00:33:10.780
so far, for the
folks who have been

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:12.440
using the second
generation runtimes,

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:14.430
it's been the number
one requested feature.

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:16.090
It's just having a cache.

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:18.400
And this delivers that.

00:33:18.400 --> 00:33:21.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

